choice("Continue", B2-Ch11b-Fatigue, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11b-Fatigue)


ID: B2-Ch11b-Fatigue
TEXT:

#if(v_ch11_saved_rose == 1) {
 "How many are still inside their rituals?" Daren asks me. "Only Kate and Rose?"

"Just Kate and Rose," I say. "You can tell by the yellow light. We have to wait for everyone to clear the first stage before the next one triggers."

"I can't say I'm looking forward to it," Daren says. "Where is the God of Time? Is he watching us?"

"No, he returned to the magical plane," I say. "He'll only manifest again at the end of the ritual, according to Arraka."

While Daren and I talk, the yellow light drains from Kate's eyes as she pulls out of her trance. The grief and exhaustion on her face are wrenching to watch—not just sorrow and regret, but the hollow resignation showing just how deep the trial cut into her. Realizing she is no longer alone, Kate instantly pulls up a mask of dull, extreme fatigue.

"Is everyone else finished?" Kate asks, her voice flat. "Where is the God of Time? I thought he was supposed to oversee these trials."

"The first stage is done," Daren says. "The God of Time went back to the magical plane and will only return at the conclusion."

"If that's true, maybe we can move against him," Kate says, looking between us. "Maybe we can find a way to sabotage the ritual while he isn't watching..."

"You can't sabotage the rituals," Melindra says from the edge of the clearing. "Eiden tried and failed in the past. If he couldn't crack them, how could we?"

"Eiden isn't all-knowing," Daren says. "Just because he failed doesn't mean we will."

"Oh?" Melindra crosses her arms. "And how do you propose to begin this 'sabotage'?"

"We'll... figure something out..." Daren mutters.

"If you say so," Melindra says.

The light fades from Rose's eyes as she finishes her ritual as well. She stands rigid, staring blankly ahead without acknowledging us, her face pale and completely horrified.

"Rose..." Daren says, stepping toward her with deep concern. "Are you all right?"

Rose gives no sign of having heard him, staring into empty space in absolute silence.

}
#if(v_ch11_saved_rose == 0) {
 "How many are still inside their rituals?" Daren asks me. "Only Kate?"

"Just Kate," I say. "You can tell by the yellow light. We have to wait for everyone to clear the first stage before the next one triggers."

"I can't say I'm looking forward to it," Daren says. "Where is the God of Time? Is he watching us?"

"No, he returned to the magical plane," I say. "He'll only manifest again at the end of the ritual, according to Arraka."

While Daren and I talk, the yellow light drains from Kate's eyes as she pulls out of her trance. The grief and exhaustion on her face are wrenching to watch—not just sorrow and regret, but the hollow resignation showing just how deep the trial cut into her. Realizing she is no longer alone, Kate instantly pulls up a mask of dull, extreme fatigue.

"Is everyone else finished?" Kate asks, her voice flat. "Where is the God of Time? I thought he was supposed to oversee these trials."

"The first stage is done, and now we wait for the second," Daren says. "The God of Time went back to the magical plane and will only return at the conclusion."

"If that's true, maybe we can move against him," Kate says, looking between us. "Maybe we can find a way to sabotage the ritual while he isn't watching..."

"You can't sabotage the rituals," Melindra says from the edge of the clearing. "Eiden tried and failed in the past. If he couldn't crack them, how could we?"

"Eiden isn't all-knowing," Daren says. "Just because he failed doesn't mean we will."

"Oh?" Melindra crosses her arms. "And how do you propose to begin this 'sabotage'?"

"We'll... figure something out..." Daren mutters.

"If you say so," Melindra says.

"If you're looking to sabotage the ritual, then I might be able to offer some assistance," Rose's voice cuts in quietly.

We had all been ignoring the revenant until this point, but now every head snaps toward her at the perimeter.

"And why would we want help from you, revenant?" Daren says, disgust hardening his voice.

"Because this ritual is as much a problem for me as it is for you," the revenant says calmly. "...And also because as much as I've tried to avoid it, traces of your friend Rose within me will always be a part of who I am. At the very least, I know Rose would have wanted us to work together, despite our differences, since nothing would have been more important to her than ensuring you are safe."

}
choice("Continue", B2-Ch11c-Herbalist, v_current_scene = B2-Ch11c-Herbalist) if (v_ch11_saved_rose == 1)
